@using MudBlazor

<div class="d-flex flex-column">
    <div class="pa-3" style="max-height:70vh; overflow-y:auto">
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-3">
            The report reads the sprint's issues from Jira together with their change history, so every number is
            taken at a point in time rather than from today's values. Story points are used where issues are
            estimated; otherwise the same measures are shown as issue counts.
        </MudText>

        <MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle1" Class="font-weight-bold mb-2">Sprint window</MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-3">
            Start, end and completion come from the Jira sprint when Jira returns them; otherwise the team sprint's dates
            in this app are used (start of the first day, end of the last day). A running sprint is measured now, a closed
            sprint at the moment it was completed in Jira.
        </MudText>

        <MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle1" Class="font-weight-bold mb-2">Measures</MudText>
        <table class="sr-table mb-3">
            <tbody>
                <tr><td style="width:170px"><b>Committed</b></td><td>Issues in the sprint when it started, with the story points they had at that moment.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><b>Added</b></td><td>Issues pulled into the sprint after it started, including issues created in it. Scope change = added ÷ committed.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><b>Completed</b></td><td>Issues whose status belongs to Jira's <i>Done</i> category at the end of the sprint (or now, while it runs).</td></tr>
                <tr><td><b>Commitment met</b></td><td>Completed share of the commitment: completed committed story points ÷ committed story points. 80 % and above is green, 50–80 % amber, below 50 % red.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><b>Not completed</b></td><td>Issues still open at the end of the sprint. In a closed sprint these are the issues carried over.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><b>Unestimated</b></td><td>Issues in the sprint without story points. They count as 0 in every story-point measure.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><b>Blocked</b></td><td>Open issues whose status contains "block".</td></tr>
                <tr><td><b>Capacity</b></td><td>Working days of the team's active members in the sprint window minus their holidays, times the team's story points per day. Coefficients are not applied. Load = committed ÷ capacity.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle1" Class="font-weight-bold mb-2">Burndown</MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-3">
            For every day of the sprint the chart shows the story points (or issues) of the issues that were in the sprint
            and not done at the end of that day. <i>Scope</i> is everything in the sprint that day, so a growing gap between
            the two lines means work was added. <i>Ideal</i> runs from the commitment to zero over the working days
            (Monday to Friday) of the planned sprint.
        </MudText>

        <MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle1" Class="font-weight-bold mb-2">Velocity</MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-3">
            The previous sprints of the team are loaded the same way. The average velocity counts the completed story points
            of closed sprints only.
        </MudText>

        <MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle1" Class="font-weight-bold mb-2">Limits</MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2">
            Issues removed from a running sprint no longer match the sprint in Jira, so they are not listed; Jira's own sprint
            report shows them. Sub-tasks appear only when they are in the sprint themselves. Reports are cached for ten minutes
            (an hour for closed sprints); Refresh reads Jira again.
        </MudText>
    </div>
</div>
